    "alt": "Business owner and candidate reviewing a role record at an office table beneath the Recruitment Strategies title.",
    "imageCaption": "AI-created editorial illustration with topic title. Numerical cases in this guide are fictional.",
    "html": "<p>All numerical examples are original fictional USD teaching cases. They are not market rates, customer results, eligibility determinations or forecasts. Replace assumptions with dated records.</p><h2 id=\"role-first\">Start with work that a new hire can actually take over</h2><p>List the recurring tasks, quality standard, required schedule and decision authority before writing an advert. A founder who needs production support may still be the bottleneck for approvals. Hiring another producer will not remove an approval queue unless the role and handoff address it. Separate an enduring workload gap from a temporary deadline or an inefficient process.</p><p>For an original agency case, signed work requires 240 delivery hours next month and the current team can provide 180. The initial gap is 60 hours. A proposed hire has 160 paid monthly hours, but the plan allows 40 for onboarding, 30 for internal work and 10 for leave or other downtime. Planned delivery capacity is only <strong>80 hours</strong>. If the founder must supervise for 20 hours that would otherwise support delivery, the net increase is 60, exactly the stated gap. This is a capacity hypothesis, not a promise of first-month productivity.</p><h2 id=\"channels\">Choose channels for the role and document the comparison</h2><p>Employee referrals can reach people familiar with the work; targeted job boards can reach a defined skill group; general job boards widen reach; schools, workforce organizations and professional communities may support different experience levels. A specialist recruiter adds an external service and contractual cost. An internal move may shorten some onboarding but create another vacancy. None is universally the cheapest or best source.</p><p>Publish the same essential role requirements and evaluate the relevant work evidence consistently. The EEOC’s small-business guidance addresses consistent screening and nondiscriminatory hiring. A referral is an introduction, not a reason to lower the standard or ignore other qualified applicants. Establish an accessible contact route for application-process needs and use current guidance for the rules that apply.</p><h2 id=\"funnel\">Compare qualified hiring outcomes rather than raw applications</h2><p>Suppose one role is advertised in two channels over the same four-week window. Counts below are original assumptions, not channel benchmarks. “Qualified” means meeting the role’s documented criteria, and a start means the person actually begins work. Keep the same cost boundary in both rows.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original recruitment funnel; illustrative USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Channel</th><th scope=\"col\">Applicants</th><th scope=\"col\">Qualified</th><th scope=\"col\">Interviews</th><th scope=\"col\">Starts</th><th scope=\"col\">External cash cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Targeted board</th><td>40</td><td>12</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>$600</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Referral program</th><td>10</td><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>$500</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The board’s qualified share is 12 ÷ 40 = 30%, versus 5 ÷ 10 = 50% for referrals. Cash cost per start is $600 and $500, respectively. Those small counts do not prove that referrals always perform better, nor that either hire will stay or deliver the work. A second observation window and role-specific outcomes are more informative than treating two starts as a universal ranking.</p><h2 id=\"full-cost\">Include internal recruiting and onboarding time once</h2><p>The example team also spends 20 interview and administration hours valued at $30 each, adding $600 allocated internal cost. Combined recruiting cost is $600 + $500 + $600 = $1,700, or $850 across two starts. External cash spending is still $1,100: the allocated time is not automatically an additional bank payment. Record whether that time was existing salary, overtime or outside contractor work.</p><p>Then budget onboarding separately. If a new employee earns $4,000 in a month, that entire payroll commitment still exists even when only 80 of 160 paid hours are productive. Do not add the same salary again as an “onboarding loss” in the cash budget. Use <a href=\"/blogs/cost-per-hire-vs-employee-cost/\">cost per hire versus employee cost</a> and the <a href=\"/calculators/employee-cost-calculator/\">employee cost calculator</a> for their separate cost boundaries.</p><h2 id=\"selection\">Use a consistent selection record and a realistic offer</h2><p>Translate the role into a small set of observable criteria: for example, accuracy of a work sample, explanation of a relevant process, schedule fit and experience needed for a specific duty. Ask comparable job-related questions and retain evidence supporting the evaluation. Do not turn an informal “culture fit” label into a substitute for the requirements.</p><p>Explain the scope, pay structure, reporting relationship and expected ramp-up before acceptance. Screening, accommodations, background checks and record retention have legal requirements that depend on the facts and jurisdiction; the linked EEOC material is a starting point, not a promise that this illustrative process establishes compliance. Keep applicant identifiers and sensitive details out of a public planning worksheet.</p><h2 id=\"review\">Review the hire at 30, 60 and 90 days</h2><p>Agree in advance what successful independent work looks like. Track whether the employee started, completed the relevant training, met the work standard and stayed through the review date. Separate time-to-fill from time-to-productivity. A fast start can still need a long supervised ramp-up.</p><p>In the capacity case, if the hire supplies only 50 rather than 80 delivery hours and the founder still gives up 20, the net increase is 30. Total effective capacity becomes 180 + 30 = 210, leaving 30 of the 240 required hours unsupported. The next decision may be a scope or schedule change, not another advert. Connect the review to <a href=\"/blogs/business-growth-strategies/\">growth and capacity planning</a>, <a href=\"/blogs/employee-compensation/\">the complete compensation budget</a> and <a href=\"/blogs/payroll-small-business/\">the payroll handoff</a>. A hiring strategy is complete only when the work, cost and handoff make sense together.</p><h2 id=\"practical-record\">Build your own review record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/recruitment-strategies-small-business-worksheet.html\">Open the editable, printable Recruitment strategy review</a>. It stays in the browser, does not automatically calculate and does not save entries after reload. Print or save a copy of your completed record. Use fictional or non-sensitive planning data; this is not a payroll, tax, lending or grant submission system.</p><h2 id=\"sources-scope\">Sources and scope</h2><p>Primary references checked November 17, 2026. The original case and decision record are separate from the source guidance; follow the applicable framework, current terms and local requirements.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.eeoc.gov/employers/small-business/3-im-recruiting-hiring-or-promoting-employees\">EEOC: recruiting, hiring and promotion</a> — Consistent screening and applicable nondiscrimination responsibilities.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/ecec/home.htm\">BLS: compensation cost categories</a> — Context for distinguishing wages and benefits from recruitment expense; no benchmark rate is assumed.</li></ul>",
